today I'd like to talk about a  collaborative model today I'd like to  talk about a collaborative model for  bioinformatics education combining  biologically inspired bioinformatics  with project-based learning and this  presentation is based on a collaboration  between Pine biotech and the Loyola  University of New Orleans specifically a  new class and bioinformatics that was  organized by dr. Kim mix and other  faculty for students that do not have a  background bioinformatics but were  interested to join this independent  study and learn about basics of  bioinformatics in the context of  project-based learning so I want to  start by pointing out that there is a  dramatic gap between our ability to  generate very precise high-throughput  data and then our ability to analyze  interpret and apply that data and this  is a growing gap particularly because  more and more data is being generated  it's cheaper to generate data and it's  useful to generate data however the  typical approach is that then once the  data is generated it is being taken to  an analyst somebody that sits at a  bioinformatics Department does not know  about how the data was generated and  doesn't know why was generated and this  data is presented to them explained to  them by somebody that speaks a  completely different language and that's  why there was recently several studies  that were talking about the gap in the  skills and the basic skills that are  necessary for basic data stewardship  that they're only being taught at about  only 25% of educational programs  so the challenge is to take this  bioinformatics language the language of  data science and the data about biology  so the language of biology molecular  biology cell biology and to bring those  together into an integrated  bioinformatics research experience and  provided for  and students in an integrated way so  that they're not a data science course  in a computational course on on  programming and are and then a separate  lab experience about generating the data  and RNA and DNA and the genes but it's  an integrated course and the challenge  really consists of two major parts and  so let me go through these challenges  and then talk about how we could address  these challenges so first of all to  develop such an integrated course you  really need high quality professionals  that are willing to lead and to service  it's not enough to have people that are  willing to do bioinformatics research  and take that research to a level of  publications and grants and independent  ideas because ultimately they also need  to go back and serve the other projects  that might not be as interesting in  terms of computer science computational  biology and bioinformatics the second  one is really the costs of establishing  and maintaining computational  infrastructure so a bioinformatics  department really services a lot of  projects they need computational  infrastructure that is dedicated for  those purposes but that infrastructure  needs to be developed maintained and  that is costly and and you can see that  in terms of budget the two main big  portions of that typical bioinformatics  department budget is staff and  infrastructure right  and the other aspect the other big  portion of the costs and the challenges  are really around training up and  developing internal talent to be able to  address the challenges and so this is a  lack of qualified trainers that have the  experience in both computational and  biological aspects it's difficult to  find people that have the both sides of  that of that paradigm and then you have  a lack of an easy to adopt curriculum so  there's a lot of books about  bioinformatics there's a lot of courses  and technical resources out there but  there's not really a plug-and-play  curriculum  that is tested and customizable to  address specific interests and needs in  a integrated course and then also  there's an intimidating list of  prerequisites so if a student is  potentially interested he's faced with  knowing statistics and then knowing our  being proficient in our and then knowing  how to script in Linux and all of these  different requisites and for a biologist  that's a major hurdle a lot of times  people tend to choose not to even take a  course in bioinformatics because of  these prerequisites so how can we  address these challenges and this need  and prepare something that is scaleable  that is practical and that really  develops good skills for students so  first of all it's learning by doing  right so these are some principles that  we came up with that became a part of  our course that we are piloting now at  University of Loyola number one learn by  doing so dive into bioinformatics from  the very first day you start doing  bioinformatics taking datasets  analyzing datasets interpreting datasets  number two online discussions so it's  very difficult to bring people together  it's requires a lot of time but people  do spend a lot more time online today  and so we enabled this process of  discussions to share experiences and  issues with analysis to be able to ask  those questions of experience by  informaticians and review that and  reflect on their doing process then its  ability to generalize so not only some  niche project that you focus on and you  only do that project for two years its  ability to learn skills and one set of  projects and then apply them to other  types of projects where you understand  both the opportunities and the  limitations of the approaches that you  have been doing and then finally it is  applying those skills that you have  developed in a new way  to understand real-life situations and  that is to have a continuity and after  your project after you've done a project  a test project an educational project to  be able to launch your own research and  I think you know bioinformatics provides  a unique opportunity it's a very  different setting than let's say  molecular biology where you have to  collect samples and prepare those  samples and then do something really  there's a lot of data a lot of resources  out there that you can just tap into and  you can conduct your own research  because only a small portion of the data  that today is available in repositories  is fully analyzed and then of course  doing that all over again right so going  back and taking you on a different data  type learning about RNA seek learning  about DNA seek learning about mass spec  data structural biology data integration  so all of that process could continue  how do we do this technically so first  of all a big resource that is allowing  us to do this efficiently is the T Byron  for platform the platform that was  designed for scientific research allows  performing complex data processing and  analysis pipeline without coding and so  here we are really lowering the bar for  entry people without any coding  background can enter and try out very  complex and computationally intensive  pipelines run them through see what the  outputs are and understand how they  might be useful not only that the  platform is visual interactive every  button that you see it comes with a  pop-up and the pop-up both explains what  the button does the algorithm and also  provides more resources to learn about  the algorithm typically a publication  and a summary of what you will find it  useful for in the same interface that  you see here is applied for many  different data types so you have all  kinds of next-generation sequencing data  pipelines mass spec pipelines structural  biology data integration and dedicated  sections for biology  and other unique sets of challenges and  so what's good about this is that you  have a dedicated section that is  compiling the right kinds of tools  around an area or a data type or an  application for that data and for those  analyses now in parallel with this  online platform we also have a set of  online courses and it's an online  learning environment that is all focused  around bioinformatics it has a number of  courses for example transcriptomics one  so transcriptomics one would go through  how to take raw task you file x'  next generation sequencing files process  those files and get a table of  expression transcriptomics to then picks  it up from there goes over a review of  how that is done in a pipeline you can  do it and then finally text talks about  differential gene expression in t-test  statistics and what that means and  transcriptomics three machine learning  for transcriptomics data how do you take  the table of expression and work with  the table of expression to classify  samples or to know which features are  most informative for classifications of  feature selection etc and so all of  these are central centered around an  individual the student the student sees  the progress can monitor their test  results can see how he compares to other  participants in a course and then of  course we have an online chat and so any  questions or challenges he can ask the  person and the person as a trained by  informatician that will answer it from  an experience perspective now these  basic skills prepare students to take on  projects and here you see in a number of  example projects that we've prepared  these projects have curated data sets  that were designed around specific  skills and they also expose the student  to a real research environment so not  only do you have the data you have the  publication about the data you need to  be able to learn from that publication  and so for example when you take a  project like this  modeling precision treatment of breast  cancer using multiple types of cell  lines and therapeutic agents what you  really get here is a data set that is  fairly complex it was taken from a  number of other publications and then  published as a meta-analysis and so we  offer a small portion of that as an  educational data set and then provide  the full project and that project could  be done independently by a student with  their own research question behind it  currently we are doing workshops and we  are preparing other projects and other  courses that are published online a lot  of these resources are free some of them  are paid and some of them are done in  collaboration with other university  researchers and Industry and now we are  giving these workshops and guest  lecturers presenting the opportunity to  implement this curriculum design it in  such a way that it fits the needs of  different levels of students we have  over 2,000 students enrolled online we  presented the solution in a number of  places including Georgetown and weiu a  couple of universities in Nebraska here  in Louisiana at LSU Tulane University of  New Orleans and we have been selected to  present at various startup competitions  at the same time I want to mention that  April 17 and 20 we are starting our  online workshops the first workshop is  going to be at University of Nebraska  Medical Center and it's going to be  jointly certified as a participant as a  participation certificate it's going to  be a three-hour workshop on  next-generation datas sequencing  analysis data analysis from NGS data and  it will touch on topics like processing  data quality control of the data  analyzing the transcript the  transcriptome analyzing the expression  levels using machine learning methods  and then also looking at various  biomedical applications of  the data analysis and the following  workshop that we're gonna have is going  to be at university of nebraska-lincoln  all the same topics but more catering to  engineering students finally I want to  point out that we have this pilot and  dr. Kim mix who has been leading this  pilot with over 12 students from various  backgrounds in various departments and  she has an exciting program that  combines various aspects of molecular  biology and genetics and incorporating  data science for those students as a way  to prepare them for careers after they  complete the course and we are very  excited about this project it's still an  ongoing pilot but all the students have  shown interest in continuing to learn  about bioinformatics continuing to learn  bioinformatics and after completing the  course they will actually present their  independent project to the faculty for  evaluation and for feedback so this is  in short our approach to bioinformatics  our approach to incorporating  bioinformatics into a biology curriculum  I think this is a very flexible way it  could also be adapted to the research  interests of faculty so that faculty  research and education go hand in hand  and I'm constantly posting more  information about these projects and  others and you can find Pine biotech on  all the social media platforms as well  as you can look at some of these free  courses that are available online  just visit edu dot T - bio info thank  you

